---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../styles/tokens.css";
import "../styles/shell.css";
import "../styles/doc.css";
import "../styles/page.css";
---
<Base
  title={"Contact · Rabta"}
  description={"Report a bug, ask for a feature, or raise something security-sensitive. Where each kind of message should go, and what happens to it."}
  route="/contact/"
  ogDescription={"Report a bug, ask for a feature, or raise something security-sensitive."}
>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="page-head__label">Contact</p>

    <h1>Talk to a human.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      No support desk, no ticket queue. Rabta is built and maintained by one
      person, so every channel below lands in the same place. What differs is
      how quickly it gets read and what shape it needs to be in.
    </p>
    <p class="mono muted">Sammy Almuflahi · maintainer</p>
  </div>

  <section class="band" data-chapter aria-labelledby="channels">
    <div class="band__inner">
      <div class="band__body">
        <h2 id="channels">Three places, and the difference between them.</h2>

        <div class="grid3">
          <!-- The whole card is the link. A card whose title, description and
               destination are one thought should have one target, not a small
               anchor inside a large rectangle that does nothing. -->
          <a class="card channel" href="https://github.com/salmuflahi/rabta/issues/new" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
            <p class="card__eyebrow">Bugs &amp; crashes</p>
            <h3>Open an issue</h3>
            <p>
              Something broke, restored wrong, or refused when it shouldn't
              have. The three things that make a report actionable are your
              macOS version, which connectors were running, and what you
              expected instead.
            </p>
            <span class="channel__action">github.com/salmuflahi/rabta/issues <span class="channel__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
            <span class="channel__where">github.com/salmuflahi/rabta/issues</span>
          </a>

          <a class="card channel" href="https://github.com/salmuflahi/rabta/discussions" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
            <p class="card__eyebrow">Questions &amp; ideas</p>
            <h3>Start a discussion</h3>
            <p>
              Workflow questions, connector requests, or how something works.
              Public and searchable, so the next person finds the answer too.
              The most useful requests describe the situation you were in, not
              the feature you designed.
            </p>
            <span class="channel__action">github.com/salmuflahi/rabta/discussions <span class="channel__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
            <span class="channel__where">github.com/salmuflahi/rabta/discussions</span>
          </a>

          <a class="card channel" href="mailto:support@rabta.build">
            <p class="card__eyebrow">Everything else</p>
            <h3>Email us</h3>
            <p>
              Press, licensing, partnerships, or anything that doesn't belong in
              a public thread. Bugs and feature requests are still better as
              issues: they stay visible to everyone else who hits the same
              thing, and they don't get lost in a thread.
            </p>
            <span class="channel__action">support@rabta.build <span class="channel__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
            <span class="channel__where">support@rabta.build</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" data-chapter aria-labelledby="security">
    <div class="band__inner">
      <div class="band__body">
        <h2 id="security">Something security-sensitive.</h2>
        <p>
          Please don't open a public issue for it. Use GitHub's
          <a href="https://github.com/salmuflahi/rabta/security/advisories/new" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">private vulnerability reporting</a>,
          which reaches the maintainer without disclosing anything publicly, or
          email <a href="mailto:support@rabta.build">support@rabta.build</a>
          directly. A fix ships before the details go public.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rabta's whole claim is that nothing leaves your machine. If you find a
          case where that is not true, that is the most valuable message this
          project can receive, and it will be treated that way.
        </p>

        <p class="mono muted foot-note">
          Response times are a person's, not a company's. Security reports come
          first; everything else gets read in order. No autoresponders: replies
          usually land within a couple of days.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--close" data-chapter aria-labelledby="elsewhere">
    <div class="band__inner">
      <div class="band__body">
        <h2 id="elsewhere">One canonical home.</h2>
        <p>
          The source, the releases and the issue tracker all live on
          <a href="https://github.com/salmuflahi/rabta" rel="me noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub</a>.
          That is the canonical home; anything else is a mirror of what is
          there.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a restore did something surprising, the Activity view records what
          each connector was asked to do and how it answered. That log is local
          to your machine, nothing is sent anywhere, so pasting the relevant
          part into an issue is usually the fastest route to a fix.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
